\details{
For generalized additive models (\code{mgcv::gam} and \code{mgcv::bam}), the
metrics are the adjusted \emph{R}^2^ (\code{"R2_adjusted"}), the percentage of the
deviance explained (\code{"Deviance_explained"}), the number of observations
(\code{"n"}), \code{"AIC"}, and \code{"BIC"}, as reported by \code{summary()}. The defaults are
\code{"R2_adjusted"}, \code{"Deviance_explained"}, and \code{"AIC"}.

For structural equation models and confirmatory factor analyses fit with
\code{lavaan} (e.g., \code{lavaan::cfa()} or \code{lavaan::sem()}), the default metrics
are the model \eqn{\chi^2}{chi^2} test (\code{"Chi2"}, \code{"Chi2_df"}, \code{"p_Chi2"}), the
comparative fit index (\code{"CFI"}), the Tucker-Lewis index (\code{"TLI"}), the root
mean square error of approximation with its confidence interval
(\code{"RMSEA"}, \code{"RMSEA_CI"}), and the standardized root mean square residual
(\code{"SRMR"}). \code{"AIC"} and \code{"BIC"} are also available, and \code{"all"} selects
every metric. Values come from \code{\link[lavaan:fitMeasures]{lavaan::fitMeasures()}}. When the model was
fit with a robust estimator (e.g., \code{estimator = "MLR"} or \code{"WLSMV"}), the
scaled \eqn{\chi^2}{chi^2} test and the robust (or, if unavailable, scaled) CFI,
TLI, and RMSEA are displayed. \code{\link[=apa_note]{apa_note()}} gives the sample size, the
estimator, and how missing data were handled, and defines the
abbreviations.

For meta-analyses (\code{metafor::rma()}), the metrics are the number of
studies (\code{"k"}), the test of residual heterogeneity (\code{"QE"} and \code{"p_QE"},
with its \emph{df} in the header), \eqn{\tau^2}{tau^2} (\code{"tau2"}) and
\eqn{\tau}{tau} (\code{"tau"}), \eqn{I^2}{I^2} (\code{"I2"}), \eqn{H^2}{H^2}
(\code{"H2"}), confidence intervals of \eqn{\tau^2}{tau^2} and \eqn{I^2}{I^2}
(\code{"tau2_CI"} and \code{"I2_CI"}, from \code{\link[stats:confint]{stats::confint()}}), and, for
meta-regressions, the omnibus test of the moderators (\code{"QM"} and \code{"p_QM"})
and the proportion of heterogeneity they explain (\code{"R2"}). The defaults are
\code{"k"}, \code{"QE"}, \code{"p_QE"}, \code{"tau2"}, and \code{"I2"}, plus \code{"QM"}, \code{"p_QM"}, and
\code{"R2"} for meta-regressions. Equal-effects models have no
\eqn{\tau^2}{tau^2}.

For multilevel meta-analyses (\code{metafor::rma.mv()}), \code{"k"} is the number of
effect sizes, and the variance components are reported for each level:
\eqn{\sigma^2}{sigma^2} (\code{"sigma2"}), \eqn{\sigma}{sigma} (\code{"sigma"}), and
their confidence intervals (\code{"sigma2_CI"}), with the level as a subscript.
\code{"I2"} is the multilevel \eqn{I^2}{I^2} for all levels together, and
\code{"I2_levels"} gives it for each level (Cheung, 2014). The defaults are
\code{"k"}, \code{"QE"}, \code{"p_QE"}, \code{"sigma2"}, and \code{"I2"}, plus \code{"QM"} and \code{"p_QM"}
for meta-regressions.

For multinomial logistic regression models (\code{nnet::multinom}), \code{R2} is
McFadden's pseudo-\emph{R}^2^, and the default metrics are \code{R2} and \code{AIC}.

For zero-inflated and hurdle models (\code{pscl::zeroinfl} and \code{pscl::hurdle}),
\code{R2} is computed by \code{\link[performance:r2_zeroinflated]{performance::r2_zeroinflated()}}, and the default
metrics are \code{R2} and \code{AIC}.

For \code{glm} objects, \code{R2} is the pseudo-\emph{R}^2^ that \code{\link[performance:r2]{performance::r2()}} selects for the model family (e.g., Tjur's \emph{R}^2^ for logistic regression and Nagelkerke's \emph{R}^2^ for count models). The header names the pseudo-\emph{R}^2^ that was used. Because the residual standard deviation is not meaningful for most GLMs, the default metrics are \code{R2} and \code{AIC}.

For mixed models (\code{lme4::lmer}, \code{lme4::glmer}, \code{glmmTMB::glmmTMB}, and \code{nlme::lme}), the default metrics are the marginal \emph{R}^2^ (variance explained by the fixed effects), the conditional \emph{R}^2^ (fixed and random effects), and the intraclass correlation (ICC), as computed by \code{\link[performance:model_performance]{performance::model_performance()}}.

For a list of models, each model's metrics are displayed in a row, labeled in a \code{Model} column by the list's names (or "Model 1", "Model 2", ... for unnamed elements). If \code{metrics} is \code{NULL} (default), each model's default metrics are used.
